  "problem": "Find the solution to the inequality $1<\\frac{x-2}{3}<2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$\n{{field-3}}\n$8$",
  "worked_solution": "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we multiply by $3$ to eliminate the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & <\\frac{x-2}{3}<2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & <3\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{3}<3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & <x-2<6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue isolating $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & <x-2<6 \\\\ 3+2 & <x-2+2<6+2 \\\\ 5 & <x<8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $5<x<8.$",
